### Place an order carrying a trace. The same trace id turns up at the receiver seconds later.
###
### If the id below is NOT the one you see in the database, your HTTP client is injecting its own
### traceparent alongside this one. Two traceparent headers are unparseable, so ASP.NET Core drops
### both and starts a new root trace. Send `GET /trace` from the same client: if
### incomingTraceParentCount is 2, that is the cause. See README section 7.
POST http://localhost:5008/orders
Content-Type: application/json
traceparent: 00-4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736-00f067aa0ba902b7-01

{ "tenantId": "acme", "customerId": "cust-ambient", "total": 42.00 }

### Override the ambient trace with an explicit one. Both are sent; the explicit one wins.
POST http://localhost:5008/orders
Content-Type: application/json
traceparent: 00-11111111111111111111111111111111-2222222222222222-01

{
  "tenantId": "acme",
  "customerId": "cust-explicit",
  "total": 7.00,
  "traceParent": "00-aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa-bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb-01"
}

### Place an order with no trace at all. ASP.NET Core still starts a root trace, so traceParent
### comes back populated - with a brand new trace id rather than null.
POST http://localhost:5008/orders
Content-Type: application/json

{ "tenantId": "acme", "customerId": "cust-untraced", "total": 1.00 }

### Inspect the persisted trace context.
GET http://localhost:5008/outbox/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

### The trace the current request belongs to.
GET http://localhost:5008/trace
traceparent: 00-4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736-00f067aa0ba902b7-01

### What the receiver saw, traceparent header included.
GET http://localhost:5100/deliveries
